= Kolb's experiential learning cycle — Technical =

== Provenance and theoretical structure ==
David A. Kolb synthesized earlier work on experience, reflection and development, notably associated with John Dewey, Kurt Lewin and Jean Piaget, in ''Experiential Learning: Experience as the Source of Learning and Development'' (1984). His proposal treats learning as a process in which experience is apprehended or comprehended and then transformed by reflection or action.<ref name="kolb1984">Kolb, David A. (1984). ''Experiential Learning: Experience as the Source of Learning and Development''. Prentice Hall. ISBN 9780132952613.</ref> These historical influences are not interchangeable theories; Kolb's synthesis is his own model, not a finding that all predecessors endorsed the four-stage diagram.

== Two conceptual dimensions ==
The model distinguishes '''grasping experience''' (concrete experience, CE, versus abstract conceptualization, AC) and '''transforming experience''' (reflective observation, RO, versus active experimentation, AE). In its familiar teaching representation the modes appear as CE → RO → AC → AE → renewed CE. The arrows depict an interpretive idealization, not a measured invariant temporal sequence. The tension between concrete and abstract and between reflection and action is central to the theoretical account. Kolb and Kolb's later account situates the cycle within a learning environment and discusses adaptations to the theory; it is a primary theoretical elaboration, not an independent trial of the four-stage sequence.<ref name="kolb2005">A. Y. Kolb and D. A. Kolb, [https://doi.org/10.5465/amle.2005.17268566 Learning Styles and Learning Spaces: Enhancing Experiential Learning in Higher Education], ''Academy of Management Learning & Education'' 4, 193–212 (2005).</ref><ref name="coffield2004">Coffield, Frank; Moseley, David; Hall, Elaine; Ecclestone, Kathryn (2004). ''Learning styles and pedagogy in post-16 learning: A systematic and critical review''. Learning and Skills Research Centre. https://blogs.northampton.ac.uk/learntech/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2016/06/Coffield-2004-Learning-styles-and-pedagogy-in-post-16-learning.pdf</ref>

=== Modes and possible observations ===
{| class="wikitable"
! Mode !! Educational prompt !! Possible evidence of engagement !! Important caveat
|-
| CE || What happened in a situated task? || Task record or observed event || Participation alone does not show learning
|-
| RO || What patterns or discrepancies were noticed? || Comparison of observations and interpretations || Retrospective accounts can be biased
|-
| AC || What explanation could account for the observations? || Explicit provisional model or prediction || A plausible explanation can be false
|-
| AE || What new test could discriminate explanations? || Planned intervention and recorded outcome || A single trial seldom establishes causation
|}

== Application as an instructional design heuristic ==
A course designer can pair authentic practice with structured debriefing, conceptual resources and opportunities to test revised understanding. For example, a clinical simulation can produce a concrete encounter, a guided debrief can elicit observations, a physiological model can help explain the observations, and a subsequent simulation can test a changed procedure. This is an '''illustrative mapping''', not proof that implementing the four-mode sequence improves clinical outcomes. The AMEE Guide discusses both applications and limitations in health-professions education.<ref name="amee2012">Yardley, Sarah; Teunissen, Pim W.; Dornan, Tim (2012). Experiential learning: AMEE Guide No. 63. ''Medical Teacher''. doi:10.3109/0142159X.2012.650741. https://doi.org/10.3109/0142159X.2012.650741</ref>

== Separate measurement claims: learning styles ==
Kolb's Learning Style Inventory operationalizes relative preferences for learning modes; the familiar four style names are ''diverging'' (CE/RO), ''assimilating'' (AC/RO), ''converging'' (AC/AE) and ''accommodating'' (CE/AE). The instrument and its psychometric assumptions are distinct from the descriptive cycle. Ranking or ipsative response formats can constrain scores, and categorization may obscure within-person variation. The systematic review by Coffield and colleagues scrutinized the conceptual and empirical bases of learning-style inventories, including Kolb's.<ref name="coffield2004" />

Crucially, '''describing differences in self-reported preference does not establish an instructional interaction'''. A claim that matching a student's instruction to an assessed style improves learning requires a comparison of matched and mismatched instruction with common outcome measures and appropriate assignment. No such causal benefit follows merely from the cycle diagram or an inventory score. To support the matching hypothesis, randomly allocate instructional methods within independently measured preference groups, use a common assessment, and test the predicted style-by-method crossover rather than merely a main effect or a preference correlation. Pashler and colleagues found little evidence meeting this design criterion; this conclusion does not rule out useful experiential teaching methods.<ref name="pashler">H. Pashler, M. McDaniel, D. Rohrer and R. Bjork, [https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2009.01038.x Learning Styles: Concepts and Evidence], ''Psychological Science in the Public Interest'' 9, 105–119 (2008).</ref> The evidence concerning learning-style matching is substantially weaker than many training materials imply.

== Criticism, scope and uncertainty ==
The cycle's orderly diagram may conceal simultaneous processes, discontinuities, unequal access to experiences, social feedback, cultural context and power relations. An experience can reinforce a misconception; reflection can be mistaken; a hypothesis can be underdetermined; and testing can be confounded. A literature review of experiential learning in medical education explicitly notes that learning can be fragmented and that the social context is insufficiently represented by simple stage models.<ref name="amee2012" />

The model is therefore best used as a '''question-generating framework''' rather than a universal law of cognition. Evaluation of an intervention inspired by Kolb should specify learners, context, comparator, measurable outcomes and follow-up; mere fidelity to four headings is not evidence of educational effectiveness. For example, compare a structured debrief and repeated practice with equivalent task exposure, instructor time and feedback in the comparator; use a delayed transfer assessment, blinded scoring where practical and report uncertainty. A positive result for the combined intervention would not isolate the contribution of each mode. The model also should not be equated with an AI agent's internal decision cycle without implementation-specific and comparative evidence.

== Sources and evidence boundaries ==
* '''Primary theoretical source:''' Kolb (1984), bibliographic attribution and the original conceptual model; the original book is the theoretical source; the 2005 author elaboration and the 2012 AMEE review clarify the model's scope.
* '''Critical systematic review:''' Coffield et al. (2004), which analyzes learning-style models and associated validity claims.
* '''Discipline-specific review:''' Yardley, Teunissen and Dornan (2012), which discusses experiential learning in medical education and its limitations.
* No universal effect size, invariant stage timing, neurological localization or quantitative formula is asserted here.

